-- statement:
--   The theorem states that for every irrational θ with 0 < θ < 1, there exists a continuous real-valued function f on the circle ℝ/ℤ (with the circle given its Haar probability measure) satisfying the predicate IsContinuousCounterexample θ f. This predicate says first that f is a continuous weight: f takes values in [0,1], vanishes exactly at the base point 0 of the circle, and the lower integral of −log|f| over the circle is infinite. It then concerns the Hilbert space of square-integrable ℓ²(ℤ)-valued functions on the circle, the operator T = U·M_f, where U is the shift applied pointwise in the ℓ²(ℤ) variable and M_f multiplies the k-th coordinate at x by f(x + kθ), and the von Neumann algebra generated by U and the multiplication V by the character e^{2πix} along the same orbit weights. The remaining requirements are that T lies in this algebra; that the only projections p in the algebra with (1 − p)Tp = 0 are 0 and 1; that T ≠ 0; that T is norm quasinilpotent, meaning ‖Tⁿ‖^{1/n} → 0; and that the vector Fuglede–Kadison determinant of T at the vacuum vector, the constant function equal to the basis vector at index 0, equals 0.
